---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE, LEGAL } from '../consts';

// Exempt from the site's ASD-STE100 rules. See scripts/ste-check.mjs.
// Values come from LEGAL in src/consts.ts.
---

<PageLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="Where Suwatte keeps your data, what leaves your device, and the controls available to you."
  meta={`Version ${LEGAL.privacyVersion} · Effective ${LEGAL.privacyEffective}`}
>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>In short.</strong> Suwatte has no account or advertising. Your library is held on your
      device and in your private iCloud storage. Content requests go directly to the Sources,
      Servers, and metadata providers that you choose. The app uses Google Analytics for Firebase
      to help us understand feature use and improve reliability. We also receive information when
      you contact support or visit this website, and Apple can provide opt-in App Store diagnostics.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Who we are</h2>

  <p>
    {LEGAL.entity} ("we", "us") is responsible for the limited personal data described in this
    policy. We are based in {LEGAL.country}. Contact us at{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>{LEGAL.address ? `, or by post at ${LEGAL.address}` : ''}.
  </p>

  <p>
    This policy applies to the Suwatte app for iOS and iPadOS, this website, and support messages
    sent to us. It does not govern Apple or a Source, Server, metadata provider, or website that you
    choose to use.
  </p>

  <h2>2. What we receive</h2>

  <p>
    The app includes Google Analytics for Firebase. This sends usage events and technical data
    to Google, where we can use analytics reports to understand which features are used and where
    operations succeed or fail. The app does not include an advertising SDK or Firebase Crashlytics.
  </p>

  <p>We receive information in these circumstances:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>App analytics.</strong> Events describe actions such as setup, Source installation,
      searches, library changes, reading, downloads, backups, and settings changes. Depending on
      the action, they can include a Source identifier, content format and origin, counts,
      durations, settings categories, and success or failure categories. Search events record a
      length range rather than the search text. Our product events do not include book titles,
      book or chapter identifiers, file contents, Server addresses, passwords, or API keys.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Analytics identifiers and technical data.</strong> Firebase can collect an
      app-instance identifier, the device's vendor identifier, app and operating-system versions,
      device information, language, session activity, and approximate location derived from the
      connection's IP address. These identifiers can link events from an installation; this data
      is not anonymous merely because Suwatte has no account. We do not request GPS location for
      analytics. Ad-personalization signals and ad-network registration are disabled in the app.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Support.</strong> If you contact us, we receive your email address, message, and any
      logs or attachments that you choose to send.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Website delivery.</strong> The site contains no analytics or advertising code. Its
      host may process ordinary request data, such as an IP address, user agent, requested page,
      date, and time, to deliver and secure the site. The site stores your colour-theme choice in
      your browser's local storage.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Apple diagnostics.</strong> If you choose to share analytics with app developers in
      your device settings, Apple can provide us with privacy-protected App Store usage metrics and
      crash or diagnostic reports. Apple controls this collection and your consent.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>We do not sell personal information or share it for advertising.</p>

  <p>
    Google processes analytics data through its Firebase and Google Analytics services. Processing
    can take place outside your country. See{' '}
    <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Privacy and Security in Firebase
    </a>{' '}and Google's{' '}
    <a href="https://support.google.com/firebase/answer/6318039" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      analytics data collection description
    </a> for more information.
  </p>

  <h3>Analytics and Incognito Mode</h3>

  <p>
    Analytics is enabled in the current app version, which does not provide an in-app analytics
    opt-out. Incognito Mode pauses Suwatte's reading-progress records; it does not disable Firebase
    Analytics or requests to services that you use. Apple's Share With App Developers setting
    controls Apple diagnostics, not Firebase Analytics. Contact us at{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a> with analytics privacy questions
    or requests.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Data on your device</h2>

  <p>The app stores the data needed to provide its features, including:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>your local catalogue, library entries, collections, flags, and Read Later items;</li>
    <li>titles, descriptions, tags, cover references, and other publication metadata;</li>
    <li>reading progress, history, sessions, insights, bookmarks, notes, and reminders;</li>
    <li>Source lists, installed Source plugins and settings, and Source website data such as cookies;</li>
    <li>Server configurations and cached Server metadata;</li>
    <li>local files, downloaded chapters and publications, thumbnails, and caches;</li>
    <li>app preferences and diagnostic logs; and</li>
    <li>backup files that Suwatte creates at your request or on the schedule you select.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The app uses file timestamps, system uptime, and user defaults for file management, elapsed-time
    measurement, and preferences. It does not use these system interfaces to identify you.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Apple services</h2>

  <p>
    Suwatte uses several separate Apple services. Apple handles data in those services under your
    Apple Account and Apple's privacy terms.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Service</th>
          <th>What Suwatte uses it for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Private CloudKit database</td>
          <td>
            Syncs the app database between your devices. This includes library and publication
            metadata, Source and Server configuration, installed Source plugins, progress, history,
            sessions, bookmarks and their images or notes, feed settings, and related identifiers.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>iCloud Drive</td>
          <td>
            Holds physical files that you place in Suwatte's iCloud library or another iCloud Drive
            folder that you select.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>iCloud Keychain</td>
          <td>
            Can sync Server credentials, Comic Vine and Metron credentials, and values that a
            Source places in its secure store.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>iCloud key-value storage</td>
          <td>
            Syncs small preferences, such as onboarding state and some Source and reading choices.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Apple documents that records in a private CloudKit database are not visible to the app
    developer in the developer portal. They count against your iCloud storage and are available to
    the app while you use the signed-in Apple Account.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Services that you connect</h2>

  <p>
    The app makes these connections only to provide a feature that you configure. The destination
    receives ordinary network information, including your IP address and user agent, and handles
    the request under its own terms and privacy policy.
  </p>

  <h3>5.1 Sources and websites</h3>

  <p>
    A Source is a third-party plugin that runs on your device with network access. It can initiate requests,
    use website cookies, and use ordinary and secure storage scoped to that Source. It can send
    search requests, authentication data, library actions, or reading progress to the site that it
    supports. Install Sources only from lists that you trust.
  </p>

  <h3>5.2 Komga, Kavita, and OPDS Servers</h3>

  <p>
    Requests go directly from your device to the Server address that you enter. Suwatte can send
    credentials and reading progress to a compatible Server. Remote Servers must use HTTPS. The app
    permits HTTP only for a local-network Server and warns before sending credentials without HTTPS.
  </p>

  <h3>5.3 Comic Vine and Metron</h3>

  <p>
    If you connect one of these optional metadata providers, Suwatte can send a title, year,
    publisher, series identifier, or issue number derived from a local file or its metadata. It uses
    the response to match and enrich your local catalogue. Automatic matching is a setting that you
    can turn off.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Backups</h2>

  <p>
    A Suwatte JSON backup can contain Source-list addresses, installed-Source metadata and settings,
    title metadata, library entries, flags, collections, progress, chapter and page state,
    bookmarks, linked titles, and Read Later items. It does not contain credentials, installed
    Source plugin files, cover thumbnails, publication files, or downloaded chapter pages.
  </p>

  <p>
    A Suwatte backup is different from an Apple device backup, CloudKit sync, iCloud Drive, and
    iCloud Keychain. An automatic Suwatte backup remains on the device until you export or delete
    it. You control any location to which you export it.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Retention and deletion</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Analytics.</strong> Event and user data is subject to the retention settings of our
      Google Analytics property and Google's deletion processes. Aggregated reports can remain
      after event-level data expires. Removing the app does not itself delete analytics already
      received. You can contact us to request access or deletion. Because we do not associate an
      email address or Suwatte account with analytics identifiers, we may be unable to locate a
      particular installation's records from your email address alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Device data.</strong> It remains until you remove it in the app or delete the app.
      Files stored outside the app's container remain where you placed them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Apple services.</strong> CloudKit, iCloud Drive, key-value storage, and synchronizable
      Keychain data can outlive one local installation. Manage them through the app's individual
      controls and the iCloud controls that Apple provides for your account. Deleting the app from
      one device does not by itself erase every cloud copy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Connected services.</strong> Remove a Source, Server, or metadata-provider connection
      to stop future requests. Contact the service operator to exercise rights over data that it
      received.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Support.</strong> We retain support messages only as long as reasonably necessary to
      answer the request, maintain a record of the resolution, protect the service, or meet a legal
      obligation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Website preference.</strong> Clear this site's data in your browser to remove the
      saved theme.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>8. Security</h2>

  <p>
    Credentials are stored in the operating system Keychain and are omitted from Suwatte JSON
    backups. Private CloudKit data is protected by Apple's service security. Remote Server and
    metadata-provider connections use HTTPS; local-network Servers can use HTTP only under the
    app's local transport policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    A third-party Source has network capabilities and can be unsafe or defective. Source isolation
    reduces access between Sources, but it does not make an untrusted plugin trustworthy.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Your rights</h2>

  <p>
    Depending on where you live, you may have rights to ask what personal data we hold about you,
    request a copy, correct it, delete it, restrict or object to its use, or complain to a privacy
    authority. Write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>. Because
    Suwatte has no account, we may need more information to locate records related to a request,
    particularly analytics records. Please do not send passwords, API keys, or book files. Data
    held independently by Apple or a service you connected must be managed with that provider.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Children</h2>

  <p>
    Suwatte is not directed to children, and we do not knowingly collect personal data from a child.
    The content available in the app depends on the files and services that the user configures.
    Safe Mode hides content that third-party metadata marks as mature, but it is not a parental
    control and cannot guarantee that content is classified correctly.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Changes</h2>

  <p>
    We may update this policy when the app, website, or service providers change. The version and
    effective date at the top identify the current policy. Material changes will be called out
    where practical.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Contact</h2>

  <p>
    Send privacy questions, requests, or complaints to{' '}
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .callout {
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.4rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-sunken);
  }

  .callout p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.97rem;
  }
</style>
